An alternative to linux io uring for zero trust security

60% of Google's kernel exploit bounties targeted io_uring with attacks like ROP. Docker disabled it by default. Android disabled it. ChromeOS disabled it. Google's own servers disabled it. Zero trust security models will at this time.

Yet high-performance databases are building on io_uring as their fast I/O path. TigerBeetle, parts of ScyllaDB, and many others have deep io_uring dependencies. TB is only accepting to start under io uring (as an example i know of).

What happens when your security team says "no io_uring"? You fall back to synchronous I/O. Performance tanks.

There's an alternative that seemingly nobody i saw thus far talks about.

FreeBSD AIO + kqueue provides the same completion-based async I/O model - truly non-blocking, not thread-pool simulation & with 25 years of security hardening instead of 5 years of CVEs.

This isn't theoretical:
• PostgreSQL 18 is implementing a posix_aio backend (same architecture)
• Netflix serves 30% of US internet traffic on FreeBSD
• WhatsApp handled 2M concurrent connections per server on FreeBSD
• ZFS 2.3 added O_DIRECT support, closing the last performance gap

I've been exploring this architecture for a TigerBeetle database port. The deep-dive revealed something surprising: the technical foundation is solid, validated by PostgreSQL's core team, and production-proven at massive scale.

The question isn't "Linux vs BSD." It's: what do you do when io_uring is blocked by policy or by your own professional standards?

